MView Day 23 (2026-10-01, after midnight)
=========================================

Edit mode under zoom / rotate (user)
------------------------------------
"the status line in the top left corner for edit mode is blocked by the
x-button zoom/rotate status lines ... the term 'Edit On' remains in the
beginning of the zoom/rotate line, since edit mode is on regardless".
- TRenderer.ModeLine: "EDIT ON   |   ZOOM ..." / "... ROTATE ..." while
  edit mode is on; the plain edit line says "EDIT ON" too.
Units changed: uRenderer. Spec §9.7.

Only one instance (user)
------------------------
"add an ini entry 'only one instance' (1/0) and make it on by default. it
should prevent several instances when operating from the total commander."
- [Startup] OnlyOneInstance=1 (default). uSingleInstance (new): a named
  mutex says "one runs"; the handle of a hidden message-only window (its
  own window procedure) sits in a small named shared memory block. A second MView (MView.lpr, before it makes a
  window) sends its file or folder as a full path with WM_COPYDATA
  (tagged 'MVOP', SendMessageTimeout 5 s, so a hung MView can't hang the
  new one), brings the running one to the front and ends. No window yet
  after 3 s (just starting), or no answer: it runs on its own.
- Review (independent) before delivery: a first version received the
  message in the main form; the LCL hands messages below WM_USER to
  DefWindowProc, not to a form's message method, and going fullscreen
  makes the form's window (and handle) again. Hence the separate message
  window, which stays. The sender also allows the running MView to come
  to the front (AllowSetForegroundWindow).
- The main form gets the path (HandleHandedOver), answers at once and
  opens it right after, like a drop (but never onto the sort panel); on
  the settings screen it starts the viewer with it; no path: it only
  comes to the front.
Units new: uSingleInstance. Changed: MView.lpr, uConfig, umainform.
Spec §11, §14.

Follow Total Commander (user)
-----------------------------
Asked: can MView follow Total Commander's folder? User (TC 11.03):
"option 1 would be good enough, but it would be cool to see if mview
could be a power viewer for tc".
- Total Commander sends no notice when its folder changes, but answers
  questions. uTCFollow (new) asks a few times a second (TMView's timer,
  every 200 ms): WM_COPYDATA 'GW' with "SP" (the active panel's path) and
  "SC" (the name under its cursor), answered as WM_COPYDATA 'RW' (UTF-16)
  to a hidden message window of its own. These codes come from community
  scripts; the forum / wiki pages couldn't be read here, so: if the path
  question gets no answer, the documented WM_USER+50 (1000 = active
  panel, 9 / 10 = path line, its text minus the mask) gives the folder;
  if the cursor question gets no answer, MView says so once and follows
  the folder only.
- [Sort] FollowTotalCommander: 0 off, 1 its folder (default), 2 also the
  image under its cursor. Right-click menu "Follow Total Commander" (Off /
  Its folder / Its folder and the image under its cursor), saved at once.
- MView reacts to changes in Total Commander only, never to its own
  browsing: the first answer is just noted; another folder is opened (a
  drive root keeps its backslash); in mode 2 the image under the cursor
  is shown from the lists, or with its folder; while that folder is
  being opened, further cursor moves are remembered and the last one is
  shown when it is ready (no scan restarted on every arrow key).
  Archives, FTP and plugin paths are ignored (told from the text: no disk
  access on the window thread). A Total Commander that is busy: nothing
  more is asked in that round (no 1 s stalls).
- Review (independent) found and fixed: "C:" became "C:"; archives
  opened as folders ("Not found"); a busy Total Commander could stall a
  poll for 1.2 s; the cursor support was never asked again after one
  timeout; holding an arrow key restarted the folder scan every 200 ms.
Units new: uTCFollow. Changed: uMView, uConfig, umainform. Spec §9.7,
§14.
- Later, maybe: MView as a real Total Commander Lister plugin (F3 /
  Quick view inside Total Commander); a separate DLL, bigger work.

Leak dialogs from the second MView (user's test)
------------------------------------------------
Double-clicking images in Total Commander with OnlyOneInstance=1: the
image opened in the running MView, but each second MView showed a
heaptrc "Heap dump" dialog (debug build): 4 unfreed blocks from uIOGate's
initialization. The I/O gate is deliberately never freed (a stuck read
may still use it while the process ends); a normal run doesn't get to
the report, the early exit does.
- MView.lpr: the second MView frees IOGate before it ends (no thread
  exists yet at that point).
Units changed: MView.lpr.

Following the cursor didn't work (user's test)
----------------------------------------------
"the totalcmd line by line following ... does not respond in that way.
the dir follower works perfectly fine."
- Possible causes: Total Commander answers the cursor question with a
  full path (rejected by the first version: a name with a backslash), or
  doesn't answer it at all.
- uTCFollow: a full path is now taken as it is; and if the question gets
  no answer, the documented WM_USER+50 way: the active file list's handle
  (3), the cursor's index (1007 left / 1008 right), that line's text
  (LB_GETTEXT; the name before the first tab, "name<tab>ext" joined,
  "[folder]" skipped).
- For the next test: the D line shows "TC folder: question / path line,
  cursor: question / list "name"" (or "no answer"), so one run shows
  which way works on TC 11.03.
Units changed: uTCFollow, uMView.
- Test 2 (user): "it replies always with a single number, regardless
  which line is selected. the number represents the dir": "SC" isn't the
  name under the cursor (a count or index). The name question is "SN"
  (as also used by ALTRun, github.com/zhugecaomao/ALTRun pull 106, next
  to "SP"). uTCFollow now asks "SN"; a bare number is never taken for a
  name (then the file list is read instead).
- User: "when in either 'tc follow' mode, dir recursion should be turned
  off (independent from ini)". TMView.UseRecursive: [Navigation]
  Recursive and not following; OpenMedia uses it for the scanner and the
  navigator. Switching the mode reopens the image's folder with it.
Units changed: uMView. Spec §9.7.

