The magnifier (Phase H, G6)
---------------------------
User: "should we move onto the magnifier?" Built as decided (Day 21):
- TRenderer.Lens (TLensState: on, locked, centre, radius, Mag relative to
  the screen, sharpen 0 / 1 / 2); the mode line shows it.
- GPU: DrawLens: a black disc, the textures again with translate / scale
  around the lens' centre, the shader cuts round (gl_FragCoord) and
  sharpens (4 neighbour texels, per-tile texel size); a scissor square
  without shaders; dark / light rim (amber when locked); tiles under the
  lens uploaded first.
- CPU: DrawLens: the source pixels under the lens (+1 each side; the
  screen copy when the lens shows fewer than real pixels), SharpenBitmap
  (uFilterImage) at source resolution, enlarged (nearest from 4 x, fine
  resample below; sampled when huge), filters, round mask, rim.
- TMView: SetMagnifier (menu "Magnifier (lens)", command Magnifier, Esc);
  HandleLensMouse: moves with the mouse (not over open panels), left drag
  = magnification (sideways) / size (up = larger), cursor back to the
  centre after it; wheel = sharpening, a notch at a time; wheel click =
  lock. Full images at once while it is on. [Magnifier]
  MagnificationPercent / Size / Sharpen, written on exit.
- User during the work: "the lens should also be switched off from the
  right click menu. since the wheel is occupied for the sharpening,
  images cannot be changed when the lens is on. i think this is
  reasonable. the lens is not a casual gimmick, its use is focused." (The
  menu entry switches it on and off.)
- Review (independent) found and fixed: the tilt wheel was taken (folder
  steps); the zoom / rotate modes lost their wheel; fine wheels cycled the
  sharpening several times per notch; a drag could stick after a lost
  release (now ends when no button is down; other presses during it are
  taken); the CPU sharpened the enlarged picture (grid lines) instead of
  the image's pixels; the CPU lens could cut out millions of pixels per
  move when zoomed far out (now the screen copy, or sampling); the size
  wasn't limited to the window at start; draw order of lens and frame
  now the same on both renderers.
Units changed: uRenderer, uGLRenderer, uFilterImage, uMView, uConfig,
uCommands, uMouseProfile, uMouseEngine, umainform. Spec §9.9, §14;
Strategy G6.

End of day / where MView stands (user)
--------------------------------------
"it is amazingly useful, in particular because of the totalcmd bridge.
... i think we have reached a point where the functions are implemented,
and what is left are the microscopy-specific functions, in particular
size scale and measurement." Image editing (drawn elements, text) is out
of scope: not viewing, keyboard-reliant. In scope: the scale bar.
- Test files coming: modern Leica SP8 confocal images (the technology
  reference) and Nikon images (likely a Nikon camera body: what the user
  will use next).
- Note for the plan: a camera body records nothing about the microscope
  (objective, adapter), so its images carry no pixel size. The scale then
  comes from a calibration: a stage micrometer imaged once per objective,
  MView keeps um / px per objective and camera, and the objective is
  picked with the mouse. Leica SP8 files (LIF, or exported TIFF with their
  XML) carry the pixel size themselves.
- Idea (user): a "Versarite MView" entry in the metadata of images MView
  writes, listing what was done (crop, resize, filters, mirror, source
  file). Possible for what MView saves (PNG: a text chunk, read by
  exiftool, IrfanView, Fiji); the original files are never changed.
  To decide next session.

Fit <-> 100 % centred (user)
----------------------------
"when [toggle between fit and 100%] is used ... the image is also linked
to the mouse cursor. it should be window-centered. otherwise a small
image is dragged into the top left corner, and a very large image might
become difficult to locate."
- TMView, cmdToggleFit: 100 % with the image centred in the window
  (TRenderer.OriginalSize) instead of OriginalSizeAt(mouse). The
  command OriginalSize (its own entry in the profile) still keeps the
  point under the mouse.
Units changed: uMView, uMouseProfile (caption), uCommands (comment).
Spec §9.2.
- User: OriginalSize "should also be centered. the only function i can
  think of where the mouse cursor centered view is nice is in the global
  zoom mode (x-key)". cmdOriginalSizeAt now centres too. Zooming at the
  mouse stays: the zoom mode (X1), and ZoomIn / ZoomOut with the zones off
  (as the user asked on Day 22; with the zones on they zoom at the
  centre).

Following Total Commander: visible, and only when it runs (user)
----------------------------------------------------------------
"it works perfectly, so well that it causes problems. when it is
enabled, but the totalcmd is not used, the recursion does not work.
there is no way to spot the cause ... how can we fix this in a way that
i can see that it is in totalcmd mode, without being too intrusive".
- TMView.UseRecursive: subfolders are off only while following is set
  AND Total Commander runs (a window lookup when a folder is opened, so a
  start from Total Commander counts from the first image).
- "[TC]" in the info line (before [filtered] etc.) while MView follows a
  running Total Commander; gone when it is closed or following is off.
  Nothing on the image itself.
Units changed: uMView. Spec §9.7.
- User: "1 is great. 2 is not enough. can you make the TC bold and
  blinking?" Instead of "[TC]" in the info line: a mode badge drawn by
  both renderers (TRenderer.SetBadge / BadgeBitmap / BadgeOn): bold dark
  "TC" on an amber rounded label in the bottom right corner, blinking
  0.6 s on / 0.6 s off (TMView's timer) while MView follows a running
  Total Commander; also with the info line off. The GPU uploads it like
  the panels (once per text).
Units changed: uRenderer, uGLRenderer, uMView. Spec §9.7.
- User: "the blinking is too much... turn it off please". The badge stays
  bold and amber, steady (no blink timer).
- User: "maybe we shorten the status line by a bit and make the amber
  button permanent? transparent when off, and light blue/amber when in
  either mode of TC following?" The "TC" label is always there:
  see-through (a light outline, faint text) when MView doesn't follow
  (off, or Total Commander not running), light blue for "its folder",
  amber for "its folder and the image under its cursor". The info line
  ends before it (CPU: cut with "...", GPU: clipped).
Units changed: uRenderer, uGLRenderer, uMView. Spec §9.7.
- User: "can this button be made clickable to cycle through the options
  and select them?" A left click on the "TC" label (TRenderer.BadgeHit,
  a few px of extra room) cycles off -> its folder -> folder and cursor
  -> off, saved as the menu does; the info line names the setting (and
  says when Total Commander isn't running). Taken before the panels and
  the lens.

